csv:     section3-gamma-vs-composition.csv
source:  ../source.pdf p.9 (journal page 308), the unnumbered table in sect. 3
anchor:  Some values of
anchor:  reduction
anchor:  deduced below from curves given by

# THIS TABLE FEEDS SHIPPED CODE. src/arty/fragmentation.py carries gamma = 47
# for WDSS-1 (interpolated inside the 0.1 C -> 0.25 C segment) and gamma = 65
# for the legacy grade (anchored just under the 0.45 C row's 67). Both read the
# gamma column below, and nothing else in this table.
#
# TRANSCRIPTION IS VERIFIED. Every cell was read off source.pdf page 9 at
# 420 dpi during the Phase-2.5 admissibility gate (ledger sect. 17). This
# supersedes the pre-2026-07-25 OCR, which had gamma(0.1 C) = 32 and labelled
# the third row 0.2 C; both are wrong and both reached derivation.md before
# being corrected.
#
# carbon_pct IS DERIVED, NOT TRANSCRIBED. The page labels the rows "iron",
# "steel 0.1 C", "0.25 C", "0.45 C". The 0.0 on the iron row is this repo's
# reading of "iron" as nominally carbon-free; the page states no number.

# --- what holds -------------------------------------------------------------
# Three of the five columns are ordered, which is what catches a row swap or an
# off-by-one read of the interleaved rows.
monotonic: carbon_pct increasing
monotonic: reduction_in_area decreasing
monotonic: P_F_kg_per_mm2 increasing
monotonic: gamma increasing

# P_2 is NOT monotonic (34, 42, 45, 38) -- the 0.45 C row turns it over. That is
# on the page; do not "fix" it.
total: P_2_kg_per_mm2 == 159 within 0.5

# --- the closure, and where it holds -----------------------------------------
# CORRECTED 2026-08-03. An earlier version of this file stated that the gamma
# column does not reproduce from the page's own closure under any reading, and
# that the formula is "nearly FLAT" against a column rising x3.35. That was a
# statement about the two readings then tested, not about the closure. It is
# superseded by the sweep in
#   experiment/fragmentation-field/updates/mott-fragment-shape-closure/checks/
#     mott-1947-gamma-column-strain-reading.py
# and the verdict in that update's rebaseline-verdict.md sect. 1.
#
# The page states its own closure two lines above the table:
#
#     gamma ~ 160 P_2 / P_F (1 + s_F)
#
# with P_F "the true stress and s_F the plastic strain at fracture" (p. 307,
# anchor "where P_F is the true stress"). The table tabulates P_2, P_F and a
# "reduction in area" column, and the sentence introducing it says values "of
# P_2, P_F, s_F have been deduced below" -- so reduction in area is the
# tabulated stand-in for s_F. How RA maps to s_F is a free choice the page does
# not fix, and the choice decides whether the column closes.
#
# IT CLOSES ON THREE ROWS OF FOUR. Reading s_F as the engineering fracture
# strain under constant volume, s_F = RA/(1-RA), and evaluating the denominator
# as s_F where the printed formula reads (1+s_F):
#
#     iron    20.6 vs 20   (+3.2 %)
#     0.1 C   41.1 vs 42   (-2.0 %)
#     0.25 C  52.9 vs 53   (-0.3 %)
#     0.45 C  55.9 vs 67   (-16.5 %)   <-- the only genuine non-closure
#
# Three consecutive rows to within 3.2 % on a formula with no free parameter is
# not coincidence: the column IS computed from the tabulated P_2, P_F and RA.
# (The three readings that do NOT close, kept because ruling them out is what
# localises the defect: s_F = RA gives 55.0/56.5/55.2/47.2; s_F = ln(1/(1-RA))
# gives 36.3/43.6/45.1/40.2; 1+s_F = 1/(1-RA) gives 17.1/28.8/33.3/31.9.)
#
# THE DEFECT IS ONE ROW, AND IT IS THE 0.45 C ROW. Two independent indictments,
# neither resting on the fit above:
#   - the P_2 column runs 34, 42, 45, 38 -- it breaks its own monotone rise at
#     exactly the failing row, and P_2 = 45.5 (a monotone continuation) would
#     reproduce the printed 67 exactly;
#   - the paper's own stated length law (anchor "Thus a material with a
#     high-stress") RISES at 0.45 C where sqrt(P_F/gamma) from the printed
#     column falls monotonically. The printed 0.45 C row contradicts the trend
#     law Mott states; rows 1-3 do not.
#
# CONSEQUENCE FOR CONSUMERS. Rows bracketed by two closing rows are sourced;
# anything anchored on or above the 0.45 C row is a WORKING VALUE, not a sourced
# one. gamma = 47 is an interpolation inside the 0.1 C -> 0.25 C segment, both
# endpoints closing -- SOUND (recomputing the column and re-interpolating gives
# 46.6 against the shipped 47.1, a 1 % shift, well inside the declared 45-49
# band). gamma = 65 is anchored just under the 0.45 C row, the one row that
# fails and the top row, so it has no bracketing row above it -- SHIFTED, and
# registered blocking against src/arty/fragmentation.py.
#
# Not expressible as a passing DSL check (the closure is a per-row arithmetic
# identity, not a monotonicity or a total), so it lives in the two scripts
# above. Residuals are pinned in both, so a future re-extraction that moves a
# digit moves them too. The older script,
#   experiment/fragmentation-field/challenges/source-data-audit/checks/
#     mott-1947-gamma-and-length-closure.py
# has correct residuals for the two readings it tests, but its docstring
# generalises them to "either reading of s_F" -- registered, see that file.
